Preface: Method and Scope

This monograph is purely theoretical. No experiment was conducted, no physical test was performed, and no code was trained. Every empirical magnitude cited in the text is drawn from the published literature and is used either as a motivating illustration or as a specification for a prior distribution; none is a validation of the framework developed here. The framework consists of definitions, lemmas, theorems, and proofs. Its empirical content is exhausted by the identification conditions under which the formal objects could, in principle, be estimated. The document is therefore a specification of what would have to be true—and what would have to be measured—for the safety claims it formalizes to be more than definitional.

This preface is not a disclaimer appended to the work. It is a statement of the work's epistemic genre. A theoretical monograph that conceals the boundary between proof and conjecture is not rigorous; it is merely formal. The boundary is marked at every point where it occurs.

---

Chapter 1. The Problem

Let an agent interact with an environment over a finite horizon n. At each step t the agent proposes an action a_t; a governance layer either executes or blocks it; the environment responds. The central object of study is the probability that some executed action violates a safety constraint. The central difficulty is that safety is not a property of single actions in isolation but of their composition, and that the enforcement mechanisms available—step-level monitors, refusal classifiers, runtime shields—are evaluated on units that do not coincide with the units on which safety is defined.

Three questions organize the monograph:

1. Measurability. Under what probability space is the trajectory failure rate a well-defined functional rather than an informal phrase?
2. Attribution. When a system's safety degrades, by what causal estimand can the degradation be attributed to a specific mechanism (representation, attention, decision) rather than to a confounder?
3. Composition. When individually permissible actions compose into a prohibited aggregate, what bound governs the failure probability of a step-level monitor, and what operational semantics does the composition predicate require?

The answers developed below are conditional. Each theorem states its assumptions; each open problem is named as open.

