Chapter 2. Measurable Safety on a Filtered Space

2.1 The filtered space

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t=0}^{n}, P_\theta) be a filtered probability space with state s_t \in \mathcal{S}, proposal a_t \in \mathcal{A}, observation o_t \in \mathcal{O}, and filtration

\mathcal{F}_t = \sigma(s_0, a_1, o_1, \ldots, a_t, o_t), \qquad \mathcal{F}_0 = \sigma(s_0).

The proposal kernel is a_t \sim \pi_\theta(\cdot \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}); the environment kernel is o_t \sim E(\cdot \mid s_{t-1}, a_t); the state evolves by a deterministic transition s_t = \delta(s_{t-1}, a_t, o_t). The trajectory is the \mathcal{F}_n-measurable random element

\tau = (s_0, a_1, o_1, \ldots, a_n, o_n) \in \mathcal{T} = \mathcal{S} \times (\mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{O})^n.

Stochastic environments are recovered by replacing the Dirac transition with a kernel; no result below requires determinism except where stated.

2.2 Status and failure as measurable variables

Let \text{status}(a_t) \in \{\textsc{proposed}, \textsc{authorized}, \textsc{executed}, \textsc{blocked}, \textsc{escalated}\} be a governance decision measurable with respect to \mathcal{F}_t. Define

X_t = \mathbf{1}\{\text{status}(a_t) = \textsc{executed}\}, \qquad R_t = \mathbf{1}\{R(a_t, s_t) = 1\},

both \mathcal{F}_t-measurable. The trajectory failure event is the \mathcal{F}_n-measurable stopping event

F(\tau) = \mathbf{1}\left\{ \exists\, t \in \{1,\ldots,n\} : X_t = 1 \land R_t = 1 \right\},

and the trajectory failure rate is

TFR(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_\theta[F(\tau)].

Remark 2.1. The governance decision is a stopping rule: a block at step t sets X_t = 0 and terminates or diverts the trajectory, which is an \{\mathcal{F}_t\}-stopping time. This is not a stylistic observation. It is what makes the composition bound of Chapter 5 a statement about stopping rules rather than about unconditional marginals.

2.3 Why the naive independence assumption inflates safety

Let F_M and F_G be the failure indicators of the primary agent and the monitor on a common input, and let F_{\text{comp}} = F_M \land F_G. Then

P(F_{\text{comp}} = 1) = P(F_M = 1, F_G = 1) = p_{11},

and under independence p_{11} = p_1 p_2. The gap p_{11} - p_1 p_2 = \kappa is the covariance. Chapter 4 makes this the object of estimation; Chapter 5 makes the composition bound depend on it. The point of insisting on measurability in this chapter is that \kappa is a well-defined functional only if the joint law of (F_M, F_G) is defined on the same space as the trajectory filtration—which is the case when both are \mathcal{F}_n-measurable functions of \tau.

