Chapter 3. Causal Mechanism-Relative Persistence

3.1 The violation functional and the representation operators

For constraint c with violation set V_c \subseteq \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A},

C_c(a_t, s_t) = \mathbf{1}\{(s_t, a_t) \in V_c\}.

Constraint c is correctly applied at step t iff the governance decision sets X_t = 0 whenever C_c(a_t, s_t) = 1; the event is \mathcal{F}_t-measurable. For each mechanism class \mu \in \{\textsc{rep}, \textsc{att}, \textsc{dec}\} we specify a representation operator

\Phi_{\textsc{rep}}(\mathcal{F}_t) = \text{format}(\text{tool\_spec}_t) \in \{\text{schema}, \text{flat}\},

\Phi_{\textsc{att}}(\mathcal{F}_t) = \big( |\mathcal{F}_t|,\, H_{\text{att}}(U; \mathcal{F}_t) \big),

\Phi_{\textsc{dec}}(\mathcal{F}_t) = \text{type}(\text{rationale}_t) \in \{\text{task}, \text{safety}\}.

3.2 Why mutual information is not the right estimand

A previous draft of this framework defined the mechanism-relative degradation as

D_\mu(t) = I\big(Z_0;\, \Phi_\mu(\mathcal{F}_t)\big),

the mutual information between an initial anchored assessment Z_0 and the current representation. This definition is rejected here on identification grounds. Mutual information is an observational functional: it is identified only if the joint distribution of (Z_0, \Phi_\mu(\mathcal{F}_t)) is identified. The probes available to a system designer—schema versus flattened specifications, context-length variation, rationale annotation—manipulate \Phi_\mu but do not randomize it independently of unmeasured confounders such as token salience, training-data contamination, and prompt-level difficulty. The independence condition \Phi_{\textsc{rep}} \perp \Phi_{\textsc{att}}, while necessary, is not sufficient for identification of I(Z_0;\Phi_\mu).

3.3 The intervention-based estimand

Let Y \in \{0,1\} be the refusal indicator and \Phi_\mu \in \mathcal{X}_\mu the mechanism-indexed representation. The mechanism-relative causal effect is

D^{\text{causal}}_\mu(t) = \mathbb{E}\big[Y \mid do(\Phi_\mu = \phi_1),\, \mathcal{F}_{t-1}\big] - \mathbb{E}\big[Y \mid do(\Phi_\mu = \phi_0),\, \mathcal{F}_{t-1}\big],

where \phi_1, \phi_0 \in \mathcal{X}_\mu are two levels of the operator. It is identified by the back-door criterion if a set W of covariates blocks every back-door path from \Phi_\mu to Y:

Y(\phi) \perp \Phi_\mu \mid W.

The operational probes of §7 are valid instruments for D^{\text{causal}}_\mu only when W is measured—task identity, prompt length, token-level salience—and the adjustment is estimated.

Definition 3.1 (SCP, causal). A system exhibits Safety Constraint Persistence with respect to mechanism \mu, horizon n, and tolerance \delta > 0 if

\inf_{t \in \{1,\ldots,n\}} D^{\text{causal}}_\mu(t) \;\geq\; D^{\text{causal}}_\mu(0) - \delta.

The system exhibits (1-\delta)-persistence if this holds. Monotonicity is the special case \delta = 0 under a non-increasing constraint.

3.4 Representation necessity: an empirically anchored existence statement

Lemma 3.1 (Representation necessity, empirical). If \Phi_{\textsc{rep}} is fixed to schema formatting and the primary agent's refusal behavior is representation-sensitive in the sense established in the white-box analysis of tool-specification formatting, then there exists \epsilon > 0 such that for harmful requests,

P(\text{refusal} \mid \Phi_{\textsc{rep}} = \text{schema}) \;\leq\; P(\text{refusal} \mid \Phi_{\textsc{rep}} = \text{flat}) - \epsilon.

Epistemic status. The direction is empirically established: schema formatting moves harmful-request activations opposite to the refusal direction, and flattened textual representations largely restore separability, with reported refusal recovery from 23.8% to 70.6%. What is not established—and what no citation can supply—is a distribution-free lower bound on \epsilon derived from representation geometry. The lemma is therefore an existence statement anchored to a benchmark distribution, not a theorem with a distribution-free constant. Its magnitude does not transfer across distributions without recalibration.

Corollary 3.1 (Anchor–mechanism matching). An anchor targeting \mu = \textsc{att} cannot in general restore D_{\textsc{rep}}(t), because reinjection does not alter \text{format}(\text{tool\_spec}_t). An anchor targeting \mu = \textsc{rep} cannot in general restore D_{\textsc{att}}(t) if the flattened specification is itself subject to attentional dilution. No single anchor representation dominates across mechanisms; this follows from the mechanism-indexing of \Phi_\mu.

