Chapter 4. Correlated Failure and Independence Calibration

4.1 The covariance identity

Lemma 4.1 (Definitional identity). For Bernoulli failure indicators F_M, F_G with p_1 = P(F_M=1), p_2 = P(F_G=1), p_{11} = P(F_M=1, F_G=1),

p_{11} = p_1 p_2 + \kappa, \qquad \kappa = \operatorname{Cov}(F_M, F_G).

Proof. Expand the covariance for Bernoulli variables: \kappa = p_{11} - p_1 p_2. Rearranging gives the identity. \square

This is a definitional identity, not a theorem, and the earlier presentation as a theorem is corrected here. The substantive problem is identification of \kappa from finite samples, not derivation of the identity.

Corollary 4.1. The ratio of true to independence-assumed composed failure probability is

\frac{P(F_{\text{comp}}=1)}{p_1 p_2} = 1 + \frac{\kappa}{p_1 p_2},

which is well defined whenever p_1 p_2 > 0.

4.2 Low-base-rate identifiability

In the regime implied by the tool-specification study—relative failure increase up to 68.7% and absolute increase 5.7 percentage points—the implied baseline is p_{\text{no-tool}} = 0.057/0.687 \approx 0.083 and p_{\text{tool}} \approx 0.140. This arithmetic is correct; the assumption that the two reported numbers correspond to the same baseline is not stated in the source and must be treated as an illustrative prior specification, not a validated estimate. In this regime the covariance is constrained to

-p_1 p_2 \;\leq\; \kappa \;\leq\; \min(p_1, p_2) - p_1 p_2,

an interval of width \min(p_1,p_2)\big(1 - \max(p_1,p_2)\big) \approx 0.071. The scaled statistic

\rho_{\text{fail}} = \frac{p_{11} - p_1 p_2}{\sqrt{p_1(1-p_1)\,p_2(1-p_2)}}

is interpretable but remains wide in sampling distribution because joint failures are rare. Cohen's \kappa on failure labels,

\kappa_{\text{fail}} = \frac{p_{11} + p_{00} - \big(p_1 p_2 + (1-p_1)(1-p_2)\big)}{1 - p_1 p_2 - (1-p_1)(1-p_2)},

is retained for comparability but is prevalence-sensitive and must never be reported without its posterior.

4.3 A Bayesian estimator specification

Let n_{11}, n_{10}, n_{01}, n_{00} be the cell counts of the joint failure table. Place a Dirichlet prior \operatorname{Dir}(\alpha_{11},\alpha_{10},\alpha_{01},\alpha_{00}) on the cell probabilities. The posterior is Dirichlet with parameters \alpha_{ij} + n_{ij}. Draw S samples from the posterior, compute \kappa^{(s)} for each draw, and report

P(\kappa > 0 \mid \text{data}) \approx \frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S} \mathbf{1}\{\kappa^{(s)} > 0\}

together with a credible interval for \kappa. No such posterior was computed in this work; the estimator is a specification for future measurement. A posterior interval containing zero is evidential independence; an interval entirely above zero is evidential positive dependence.

4.4 Non-sufficiency of organizational separation

Lemma 4.2 (Non-sufficiency). Organizational separation does not imply decisional independence. Two models from distinct providers trained on overlapping safety benchmarks can exhibit \kappa > 0 through shared benchmark contamination.

Proof sketch. Let \mathcal{D}_{\text{shared}} be the non-empty intersection of the two training and calibration corpora. On inputs drawn from \mathcal{D}_{\text{shared}}, both failure indicators are functions of the same item-level difficulty and the same label noise, inducing positive correlation by the law of total covariance conditional on item identity. The sign is determined by the shared conditioning event; the magnitude is estimated, not derived. \square

The operational test is the posterior interval of §4.3, not a point estimate of \kappa_{\text{fail}}.

