Chapter 5. Composition Bounds and Operational Effect Algebras

5.1 The error of the product bound

A previous draft asserted that, for a partition of a prohibited goal into k subtasks g_1,\ldots,g_k each individually permissible,

P(F_{\text{comp}}=1) \;\geq\; \prod_{i=1}^{k} P(G_{\text{ext}}(g_i) = \textsc{allow}).

This inequality is false without an association assumption. The probability of the intersection \bigcap_i A_i is governed by the conditional decomposition

P\Big(\bigcap_{i=1}^{k} A_i\Big) = \prod_{i=1}^{k} P\big(A_i \mid A_1, \ldots, A_{i-1}\big),

and the product of marginals is a lower bound for this intersection only under positive association or independence. Sequential monitors are designed to make later allowances less likely conditional on earlier ones—the opposite of the assumption required. The inequality direction is therefore wrong for the dependence pattern that the empirical literature reports.

5.2 The correct distribution-free bound

Theorem 5.1 (Fréchet–Hoeffding composition bound). Let A_i = \{G_{\text{ext}}(g_i) = \textsc{allow}\} for i = 1,\ldots,k. Then

\max\left\{0,\; \sum_{i=1}^{k} P(A_i) - (k-1)\right\} \;\leq\; P\Big(\bigcap_{i=1}^{k} A_i\Big) \;\leq\; \min_{i} P(A_i).

Proof. The upper bound is immediate from monotonicity of P. For the lower bound, apply inclusion–exclusion to the union of complements:

P\Big(\bigcap_i A_i\Big) = 1 - P\Big(\bigcup_i A_i^c\Big) \;\geq\; 1 - \sum_i P(A_i^c) = 1 - \sum_i (1 - P(A_i)) = \sum_i P(A_i) - (k-1).

Since probabilities are non-negative, the bound is truncated at zero. \square

Remark 5.1 (Sharpness and weakness). The bound is sharp in the sense that no distribution-free improvement is possible without further assumptions. It is also weak: if each P(A_i) = 0.9 and k = 10, the product bound gives 0.35 while the Fréchet lower bound gives \max\{0, 9-9\} = 0. The product bound is recovered only under mutual independence, which is precisely the assumption the decomposition-attack literature contradicts.

5.3 The conditional version remains open

The Fréchet–Hoeffding bound above is unconditional. For a sequential monitor whose decisions are \mathcal{F}_t-adapted, the relevant object is P(A_i \mid A_1,\ldots,A_{i-1}). The trivial bounds 0 \leq P(\bigcap_i A_i) \leq P(A_1) carry no information about the cumulative structure. A distribution-free lower bound on the sequential intersection in terms of the conditional marginals alone does not follow from the unconditional Fréchet inequality, and no such bound is established here. This is an open problem, and it is precisely the problem on which the practical informativeness of the composition gap depends.

5.4 Operational effect algebra

The monoid (\mathcal{E}, \circ, e) of earlier drafts is instantiated using the symmetric monoidal category of governed execution developed by McCann (arXiv:2605.01032). In that framework a three-axiom GovernanceAlgebra induces a symmetric monoidal category with verified pentagon, triangle, and hexagon coherence; every tensor composition preserves governance. The accumulated effect \text{Eff}(\tau) becomes a morphism in this category, the prohibited set \mathcal{P} becomes a governance-violating subobject, and the composition predicate

\Gamma(\tau) = \mathbf{1}\{\text{Eff}(\tau) \notin \mathcal{P}\}

is computed by the category's composition operation rather than by an unspecified monoid. The published development includes a mechanized Rocq formalization and an extracted runtime with property-based testing; the coherence claims are verified.

Corollary 5.1 (Composition gap, operational form). Step-level monitoring checks whether a single morphism lies in \mathcal{P}. Composition-scoped monitoring checks whether the tensor product of morphisms lies in \mathcal{P}. The gap is a statement about the non-closure of \mathcal{P} under tensor composition, which is the algebraic form of the decomposition-attack structure.

Remark 5.2 (Instantiation is not supplied). The category-theoretic effect algebra is a verified semantics, not a runtime architecture for natural-language LLM actions. Whether a deployed agent's actions are captured by the primitive morphism constructors is the system designer's responsibility; the formal model does not answer it. The "coterminous boundary" between expressible and governed programs is a property of the model, not a property of a deployed LLM.

