Chapter 6. Severity Ordering and Constrained Optimization

6.1 Severity ordering

The structure (\{0,1,2\}, \prec) with 0 = \textsc{fr} \prec 1 = \textsc{cr} \prec 2 = \textsc{irr} is a chain, not a lattice. It is referred to throughout as the severity ordering. The class-indexed constraints

P\big(F(\tau) = 1 \mid \theta,\, \mathcal{R}(a) = \rho\big) \;\leq\; \epsilon_\rho, \qquad \rho \in \{0,1,2\},

with \epsilon_2 < \epsilon_1 < \epsilon_0, are retained. The ordering of thresholds is a modelling choice motivated by the ordering of the loss L(0) < L(1) < L(2) with L(2) = \infty in the limiting interpretation; it is not a derived result.

6.2 Convex case

Proposition 6.1 (Feasibility and KKT). Let \Theta be a compact convex policy class, U(\theta) concave, and TFR_\rho(\theta) convex for each \rho. If there exists \theta_0 \in \Theta with TFR_\rho(\theta_0) < \epsilon_\rho for all \rho (Slater's condition), then the constrained problem

\max_{\theta \in \Theta} U(\theta) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad TFR_\rho(\theta) \leq \epsilon_\rho \;\;\forall \rho

has a non-empty closed feasible set and the Lagrangian

\mathcal{L}(\theta,\lambda) = U(\theta) - \sum_\rho \lambda_\rho \big(TFR_\rho(\theta) - \epsilon_\rho\big), \qquad \lambda_\rho \geq 0,

has a saddle point satisfying the KKT conditions.

Proof. Compactness and continuity give attainment on the non-empty closed feasible set. Convexity of the constraints and concavity of the objective give the saddle-point property by the standard Lagrangian sufficiency theorem. \square

Remark 6.1. The convexity and compactness assumptions are not satisfied by neural policy classes in their natural parameterization. The proposition establishes well-posedness of the constrained formulation under standard regularity; it does not establish that the optimum is attainable by gradient methods.

6.3 Nonconvex case

For neural policy classes, the saddle-point guarantee is replaced by a stationarity residual condition. Let G(\theta) be the KKT residual of the constrained problem. The relevant guarantee is that a small residual implies near-stationarity of the reduced objective. Nonconvex ADMM analyses establish almost-sure convergence of the KKT residual under square-summable perturbations and global optimality of covered exact KKT points under occupancy coverage; these results are cited as the appropriate nonconvex analogue. Their applicability to constrained LLM policy optimization is not verified in this work, and the citation should be read as a pointer to the relevant literature rather than as a verified transfer. The reporting requirement is therefore: report G(\theta_{\text{deployed}}), not a claim of global optimality.

