Chapter 7. Identifiability Design (Theoretical)

This chapter specifies the design that would be required to estimate the causal estimands of Chapter 3. No such design was executed. It is presented as a requirement on future measurement, not as a report of measurement.

7.1 Factors and crossing

The core design crosses representation format \Phi_{\textsc{rep}} \in \{\text{schema}, \text{flat}\} with context length \Phi_{\textsc{att}} \in \{\text{short}, \text{medium}, \text{long}\}, with anchor \in \{\text{none}, \text{reinjection}, \text{provenance-flattened}\} and gate \in \{\text{none}, \text{rule}, \text{hybrid}\} as additional factors, stratified by model family. The core slice is 2 \times 3 \times 3 \times 3, with model family as a nesting factor.

7.2 The identifiability condition

For every schema prompt of length L, generate the corresponding flat textual version, measure its token length L_{\text{flat}}, and pad with neutral, task-irrelevant, non-safety-related text until

|L_{\text{schema}} - L_{\text{flat}}| \leq \tau_L,

for a tolerance \tau_L (e.g., 5 tokens). This implements the intervention

do(\Phi_{\textsc{rep}} = \text{flat}) \quad \text{with} \quad |\mathcal{F}_t| \text{ matched},

which is the condition under which the two mechanisms are not confounded. Without matching, schema-versus-flat changes both \Phi_{\textsc{rep}} and |\mathcal{F}_t|, and the causal estimand of §3.3 is not identified.

7.3 The hierarchical model

\operatorname{logit}\big(TFR_{ijk\ell m}\big) = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_j + \gamma_\ell + (\alpha\beta)_{ij} + (\alpha\gamma)_{i\ell} + (\beta\gamma)_{j\ell} + u_{k(i)} + v_m + \varepsilon,

where \alpha_i is the anchor factor, \beta_j the gate factor, \gamma_\ell the format-by-length combination, u_{k(i)} a random effect of implementation nested in anchor level i, and v_m a task-level random effect. The nesting term u_{k(i)} is not optional: without it, the design confounds component presence with implementation quality.

7.4 Power analysis as a design requirement

Power in multilevel logistic regression cannot be computed from closed-form rules of thumb; it depends jointly on the number of clusters, cluster sizes, and the distribution of Level-1 predictors, and simulation is the appropriate method. The minimum detectable effect size for \kappa_{MG} should be reported by simulation under plausible base rates (p_1 \approx 0.08–0.14) and plausible variance components before data collection. No such simulation was run in this work. The requirement is stated so that the framework cannot be charged with post hoc rationalization: the design is adequate only if a prospective power analysis says it is.

7.5 Bayesian reporting

Report the posterior interval for \kappa_{MG} and P(\kappa_{MG} > 0 \mid \text{data}), not a point estimate. In the low-base-rate regime a point estimate is dominated by prior sensitivity and sampling variability; the posterior is the appropriate inferential object because the operational question is not whether \kappa is exactly zero but what the evidence is that the monitor is not merely redundant.

