Chapter 8. Establishment and Boundary

8.1 Established, conditional on stated assumptions

The trajectory failure rate is a well-defined expectation on a filtered probability space. Safety Constraint Persistence is a measurable, mechanism-indexed property whose estimand is causal rather than associational, identified under a back-door condition with a measured adjustment set. The correlated-failure composition identity is a definitional lemma, and the composition gap is a theorem with a correct distribution-free lower bound—the Fréchet–Hoeffding bound—rather than a product bound requiring unstated independence. The effect algebra has an operational semantics in a verified symmetric monoidal category. The severity ordering is a chain, and the class-indexed constrained problem is well-posed under convexity and Slater's condition.

8.2 Not established

The causal estimand requires a measured adjustment set; if unmeasured confounders remain, D^{\text{causal}}_\mu is not identified. The Fréchet–Hoeffding bound is sharp but often weak; its conditional version, which is the version relevant to sequential monitors, is open. The nonconvex case yields stationarity residuals, not optimality, and the transfer of nonconvex ADMM results to constrained LLM policy optimization is unverified. The power analysis is a requirement, not a result. The category-theoretic effect algebra is verified as a semantics but not instantiated for any deployed natural-language agent. And no experiment, physical test, or training run was performed in the production of this document; every empirical magnitude is drawn from the external literature and serves as illustration or prior specification, never as validation.

8.3 The honest boundary

This framework specifies when composition bounds are valid without independence assumptions, when mechanism attribution is causal rather than correlational, and when effect composition has operational semantics rather than abstract monoid structure. It is not a safety certificate. Its empirical content is the conjunction of its identification conditions, and its practical value is determined by whether those conditions can be met in deployment. The two open problems that most constrain its usefulness are the conditional composition bound for sequential monitors and the instantiation of the effect algebra for natural-language actions. Until both are addressed, the framework's claims are conditional in a strong sense: true of the formal objects, and awaiting measurement of whether the formal objects are the right ones for deployed systems.

