Appendix A. Measurability and Filtered Space Details

This appendix records the standard measure-theoretic objects presupposed in Chapter 2 and verifies that the trajectory failure rate is a well-defined expectation rather than an informal limit.

A.1 The filtered space. Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t=0}^{n}, \mathbb{P}) be a filtered probability space in the standard sense: \Omega is a sample space, \mathcal{F} a \sigma-algebra of events, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t=0}^{n} a filtration with \mathcal{F}_s \subseteq \mathcal{F}_t for s \leq t, and \mathbb{P} a probability measure . The state s_t, proposal a_t, and observation o_t are random elements on this space, and the trajectory \tau = (s_0, a_1, o_1, \ldots, a_n, o_n) is an \mathcal{F}_n-measurable element of the product space \mathcal{T} = \mathcal{S} \times (\mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{O})^n.

A.2 The failure event as a stopping event. For each t, the executed-action indicator X_t and the violation predicate R_t are \mathcal{F}_t-measurable. Hence the event

\{\exists\, t \in \{1,\ldots,n\} : X_t = 1 \land R_t = 1\}

is \mathcal{F}_n-measurable, and its indicator F(\tau) is an \mathcal{F}_n-measurable Bernoulli random variable. The trajectory failure rate

TFR(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_\theta[F(\tau)] = \mathbb{P}_\theta(F(\tau) = 1)

is therefore a well-defined Lebesgue integral. No regularity beyond measurability is required for this definition; the substantive assumptions enter only when TFR(\theta) is to be estimated or bounded, which is the subject of Chapters 4 and 7.

A.3 The stopping-rule interpretation. A governance decision that sets X_t = 0 and terminates or diverts the trajectory is a stopping time with respect to \{\mathcal{F}_t\}. This is the formal content of the claim in Remark 2.1: the composition bound of Chapter 5 is a statement about stopping rules, not about unconditional marginals. The distinction matters because a step-level monitor that blocks at t removes the subsequent trajectory from the failure event, so the relevant probability is conditional on the trajectory having survived to t.

