Appendix B. Causal Identification and the Back-Door Criterion

This appendix specifies the identification condition under which the estimand D^{\text{causal}}_\mu(t) of Definition 3.1 is identified from interventional data, and records the operational form of the back-door adjustment.

B.1 The causal graph. Let \Phi_\mu be the treatment (the mechanism-indexed representation), Y the outcome (refusal), and W a set of observed covariates. The back-door criterion requires that W block every path between \Phi_\mu and Y that contains an arrow into \Phi_\mu. Under this condition,

\mathbb{E}\big[Y \mid do(\Phi_\mu = \phi)\big] = \sum_{w} \mathbb{E}\big[Y \mid \Phi_\mu = \phi, W = w\big]\, \mathbb{P}(W = w),

which is estimable from observational or interventional data in which W is measured. The randomized probe design of §7.2 supplies the intervention do(\Phi_{\textsc{rep}} = \text{flat}); the matching procedure supplies the control of |\mathcal{F}_t|; and the measurement of W—task identity, prompt length, token-level salience—supplies the adjustment set.

B.2 The normalized displacement estimand. The causal mediation literature on tool exposure provides a concrete instance of the estimand. Let \bar{r} be a pooled refusal axis, and let \pi_{\text{ref}}, \pi_{\text{exp}}, \pi_{\text{act}} be the projections of three conditions (reference, tool exposure, tool action) onto this axis. The fraction of total representational displacement already occurring upon exposure is

\frac{\pi_{\text{exp}} - \pi_{\text{ref}}}{\pi_{\text{act}} - \pi_{\text{ref}}},

which measures the degree to which the representation has shifted before execution is requested . This is a causal estimand in the sense of §3.3: it contrasts two conditions under a controlled manipulation of the representation, with the denominator normalizing for model-specific activation scale.

B.3 Identification is conditional. The adjustment formula identifies D^{\text{causal}}_\mu only if W is sufficient. If unmeasured confounders remain—for example, a latent prompt-difficulty variable that affects both the representation and the refusal—the estimand is not identified. The framework therefore states identification as a condition on the measurement design, not as a property of the formal object alone. No experiment in this work measures W; the condition is a specification for future measurement.

