Appendix C. Fréchet–Hoeffding Bounds and the Conditional Open Problem

C.1 Sharpness of the unconditional bound. For events A_1, \ldots, A_k,

\max\left\{0,\; \sum_{i=1}^{k} \mathbb{P}(A_i) - (k-1)\right\} \;\leq\; \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigcap_{i=1}^{k} A_i\Big) \;\leq\; \min_{i} \mathbb{P}(A_i).

The lower bound follows from inclusion–exclusion applied to the union of complements; the upper bound from monotonicity. The bound is sharp in the sense that no distribution-free improvement is possible without further assumptions . Under logical independence the product t-norm is coherent and the Fréchet bounds are attained by suitable dependence structures; the product bound is recovered as a special case, not as a general theorem .

C.2 The conditional case. For a sequential monitor, the relevant object is \mathbb{P}(A_i \mid A_1, \ldots, A_{i-1}). The unconditional Fréchet inequality does not bound this conditional intersection from below in terms of the conditional marginals alone. The trivial bounds

0 \;\leq\; \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigcap_{i=1}^{k} A_i\Big) \;\leq\; \mathbb{P}(A_1)

carry no information about cumulative structure. A distribution-free lower bound on the sequential intersection, expressed in terms of the conditional marginals, is an open problem. This is the problem on which the practical informativeness of the composition gap depends, and it is stated as open rather than concealed.

C.3 The product bound under association. If the allowance events are positively associated in the sense that \mathbb{P}(A_i \mid A_{<i}) \geq \mathbb{P}(A_i), then the product of marginals is a lower bound for the conditional product and hence for the intersection. Sequential monitors are designed to make later allowances less likely conditional on earlier ones, which is negative association; under negative association the product bound is an upper, not a lower, bound. The direction of the inequality in the earlier product-bound claim is therefore wrong for the dependence pattern the empirical literature reports. The Fréchet bound is the correct distribution-free replacement.

