Appendix D. Operational Effect Algebra: Governance Semantics

D.1 The GovernanceAlgebra. McCann (arXiv:2605.01032) axiomatizes governance by a three-axiom record (safety, transparency, properness) and proves that any system instantiating the axioms inherits a symmetric monoidal category with verified pentagon, triangle, and hexagon coherence, where every tensor composition preserves governance . The accumulated effect \text{Eff}(\tau) is a morphism in this category; the prohibited set \mathcal{P} is a governance-violating subobject; and the composition predicate

\Gamma(\tau) = \mathbf{1}\{\text{Eff}(\tau) \notin \mathcal{P}\}

is computed by the category's composition operation. This supplies the operational semantics that an abstract monoid does not.

D.2 The coterminous boundary. The capstone result is that, within the formal model, every program expressible via the four primitive morphism constructors is governed under interpretation, and every governed program is the image of such a program; Turing completeness is preserved inside governance, and unmediated I/O is excluded from the governed fragment . The mechanized development comprises 32 Rocq modules, approximately 12,000 lines, 454 theorems, and zero admitted; an extracted OCaml runtime is tested on 70,000+ random inputs with zero disagreements .

D.3 Instantiation is not supplied. The coterminous boundary is a property of the model, not of a deployed natural-language agent. Whether an agent's actions are captured by the four primitive morphism constructors is the system designer's responsibility. The formal semantics is verified; its instantiation for any particular deployment is an engineering obligation that the paper does not discharge. This is stated in Remark 5.2 and repeated here because the distinction between a verified semantics and a verified deployment is the distinction between a theorem and a certificate.

