Appendix E. Bayesian Estimation of Covariance Under Low Base Rates

E.1 The Dirichlet–Bernoulli conjugacy. Let n_{11}, n_{10}, n_{01}, n_{00} be the cell counts of the joint failure table. With a Dirichlet prior

(p_{11}, p_{10}, p_{01}, p_{00}) \sim \operatorname{Dir}(\alpha_{11}, \alpha_{10}, \alpha_{01}, \alpha_{00}),

the posterior is Dirichlet with parameters \alpha_{ij} + n_{ij}. The covariance \kappa = p_{11} - (p_{11}+p_{10})(p_{11}+p_{01}) is a nonlinear function of the cell probabilities, so its posterior is obtained by simulation: draw S samples from the Dirichlet posterior, compute \kappa^{(s)} for each draw, and report the empirical distribution of \{\kappa^{(s)}\}_{s=1}^{S}. The posterior probability of positive dependence is

\widehat{\mathbb{P}}(\kappa > 0 \mid \text{data}) = \frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S} \mathbf{1}\{\kappa^{(s)} > 0\}.

No such posterior is computed in this work; the estimator is a specification for future measurement.

E.2 Why the point estimate is inadequate. In the low-base-rate regime implied by the tool-specification study, p_1 \approx 0.083 and p_2 \approx 0.140, the covariance is constrained to an interval of width approximately 0.071. A point estimate of \kappa in this regime is dominated by prior sensitivity and by the sampling variability of rare joint failures. The posterior interval, not the point estimate, is the appropriate inferential object because the operational question is not whether \kappa is exactly zero but what the evidence is that the monitor is not merely redundant. The earlier draft's point-estimate reporting is corrected accordingly.

E.3 Prior specification. The prior should be centered on the low-base-rate regime rather than at a generic 0.5. The arithmetic p_{\text{no-tool}} = 0.057/0.687 \approx 0.083 is correct, but the assumption that the relative and absolute increases reported in the source correspond to the same baseline is not stated there. The prior is therefore illustrative, not validated: it encodes a plausible magnitude, not a measured one.

