Appendix F. Power Analysis Specification for the Multilevel Model

F.1 Why simulation is required. Power in multilevel logistic regression cannot be computed from closed-form rules of thumb: it depends jointly on the number of clusters, the cluster sizes, and the distribution of Level-1 predictors, and simulation is the appropriate method because the complexity of the calculation precludes a simple formula . Skewed continuous predictors and unbalanced binary predictors require substantially larger sample sizes than balanced or normal predictors, so generic recommendations are unwarranted .

F.2 The simulation procedure. For a candidate design, specify: (i) the number of clusters and cluster sizes; (ii) the distribution of Level-1 predictors; (iii) the true variance components for u_{k(i)} and v_m; (iv) the effect sizes for the interaction terms (\alpha\gamma)_{i\ell} and (\beta\gamma)_{j\ell}. Simulate the hierarchical model

\operatorname{logit}(TFR_{ijk\ell m}) = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_j + \gamma_\ell + (\alpha\beta)_{ij} + (\alpha\gamma)_{i\ell} + (\beta\gamma)_{j\ell} + u_{k(i)} + v_m + \varepsilon,

fit the model, and record the rejection rate for the null hypothesis of no interaction at the nominal level. Repeat over a grid of effect sizes to obtain the minimum detectable effect for power 0.80.

F.3 The \kappa_{MG} sub-experiment. For the independence calibration, the relevant power is the probability that the posterior interval for \kappa_{MG} excludes zero under a true positive \kappa. Because \kappa is a nonlinear function of the cell probabilities and the joint failures are rare, the simulation should draw from the Dirichlet posterior implied by the prior of Appendix E and count the fraction of simulated datasets in which the credible interval lies above zero. No such simulation was run in this work; the requirement is stated so that the design can be evaluated prospectively rather than rationalized post hoc.

