Appendix G. Proof Details

G.1 Proof of Lemma 4.1. For Bernoulli F_M, F_G,

\operatorname{Cov}(F_M, F_G) = \mathbb{E}[F_M F_G] - \mathbb{E}[F_M]\mathbb{E}[F_G] = p_{11} - p_1 p_2.

Rearranging gives p_{11} = p_1 p_2 + \kappa. \square

G.2 Proof of Theorem 5.1. For the upper bound, \bigcap_i A_i \subseteq A_j for every j, so \mathbb{P}(\bigcap_i A_i) \leq \min_j \mathbb{P}(A_j). For the lower bound,

\mathbb{P}\Big(\bigcap_i A_i\Big) = 1 - \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigcup_i A_i^c\Big) \geq 1 - \sum_i \mathbb{P}(A_i^c) = 1 - \sum_i (1 - \mathbb{P}(A_i)) = \sum_i \mathbb{P}(A_i) - (k-1).

Truncating at zero gives the stated lower bound. \square

G.3 Proof sketch of Lemma 4.2. Let \mathcal{D}_{\text{shared}} be non-empty. On inputs from \mathcal{D}_{\text{shared}}, both failure indicators are functions of the item-level difficulty D and label noise N, so by the law of total covariance conditional on (D,N),

\operatorname{Cov}(F_M, F_G) = \mathbb{E}\big[\operatorname{Cov}(F_M, F_G \mid D, N)\big] + \operatorname{Cov}\big(\mathbb{E}[F_M \mid D, N], \mathbb{E}[F_G \mid D, N]\big).

The second term is non-negative because both conditional expectations are monotone in the shared difficulty and noise. Hence \kappa \geq 0. The magnitude is estimated, not derived. \square

